---
/**
 * `/methodology/` — SPEC.md §6, §8.
 *
 * The public home of every formula the site computes with. Deliberately says
 * what it does not yet contain rather than presenting an empty page as a
 * finished one — the physics formulae land with the math engine, and the wind
 * tunnel's validation numbers land with the solver.
 */
import Section from '../components/blocks/Section.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../components/ui/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="Every formula Markey computes with, published rather than hidden."
>
  <header>
    <h1 class="type-title text-[clamp(1.75rem,5vw,2.5rem)]">Methodology</h1>
    <p class="type-body mt-3 max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-secondary">
      Every figure this site calculates is published as a formula, not asserted
      as a result. If you disagree with a number here, you can check the working.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section title="Units">
    <div class="type-body flex flex-col gap-3 py-5 text-sm text-ink-secondary">
      <p>
        Everything is stored in SI. Imperial figures are computed for display
        when you ask for them and are never stored or indexed, so the two can't
        drift apart. The schema only accepts SI units, which means a horsepower
        figure cannot physically be saved into the database by mistake.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Still to be published here">
    <ul class="flex list-disc flex-col gap-2 py-5 pl-5 text-sm text-ink-secondary">
      <li>
        The vehicle-dynamics model — drag, rolling resistance, top speed,
        0–100 km/h, braking distance and steady-state consumption — together
        with every default constant it uses and where that constant came from.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Markey Score: its sub-factors and their exact weights.
      </li>
      <li>
        The depreciation curve, stated plainly as a projection rather than a
        market-data claim.
      </li>
      <li>
        The wind tunnel's validation numbers against published drag
        coefficients for canonical shapes — sphere, cube, flat plate and
        teardrop — so its accuracy can be judged rather than trusted.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="pb-5 text-sm text-ink-muted">
      These are listed rather than omitted because a methodology page that
      quietly covers only the easy parts is worse than one that admits what is
      still missing.
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
